These are questions and points that need to be resolved from a standard specification point of view for autolisp-spec.

COND

What happens when there are no expression beside the test in a clause?
AutoCAD and BricsCAD specification should be checked,
the implementations should be tested.

Here is what I would expect:

(cond
 ((= 1 1)))
--> t ; the value returned by (= 1 1) ie. T in autolisp.

(cond
 (42))
--> 42 ; the valuel returned by 42, which is true.

(cond
 (nil)
 ('ok))
--> ok ; since nil is false.

(cond
  ()
  (33))
==> error () is an invalid cond clause


AND, OR

The return value appears to be boolean, not the value of the last evaluated clause (what CL does).
So they cannot be used in short-circuiting expressions.

AutoCAD and BricsCAD specification should be checked,
the implementations should be tested.

UPDATE 2026-09-29 (triage housekeeping): the "needs cad-access" block is lifted -- the CI
CAD runners (native child pipeline, cad tag) run probe jobs. Write the COND/AND/OR
edge-case probes on Linux; play them on AutoCAD + BricsCAD.

RESOLVED 2026-10-04 by measurement (probes/sources/probe-cond.lsp; AutoCAD
2022 job 16921381038, BricsCAD V26 job 16921381039). Every expectation
above holds, identically on both vendors and in clautolisp:
  (cond ((= 1 1)))         => T     (a clause without body: its test value)
  (cond (42))              => 42
  (cond (nil) ('ok))       => OK
  (cond ((setq x 5)))      => 5
  (cond (nil)) / (cond)    => nil
  (cond () (33))           => ERROR on both (AutoCAD "syntax error",
                              BricsCAD "bad argument type <NIL> ; expected
                              <LIST> at [cond]"; clautolisp its own wording)
  AND / OR return only T or nil: (and) T, (or) nil, (and 1 2) T,
  (and 'a "b") T, (or nil 3) T, (or nil 'a) T -- so they cannot stand in
  for CL's value-returning AND / OR.
The spec's COND / AND / OR entries already said so; they now carry the
examples and the verification. Closed.
